::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which unit measures time?
{
=A. minutes#Correct. Minutes measure time, so the unit matches the dimension.
~B. inches#Inches measure length. Check what the unit describes before solving.
~C. gallons#Gallons measure volume. They do not describe time.
~D. pounds#Pounds measure weight. Use time units for time measurements.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 3 yards to feet.
{
~A. 3 feet#This keeps the same number without converting. Each yard contains 3 feet.
~B. 6 feet#This doubles instead of using 3 feet per yard. Use the correct conversion factor.
=C. 9 feet#Correct. Since 1 yard = 3 feet, 3 x 3 = 9 feet.
~D. 36 feet#This uses 12 as if converting feet to inches. First convert yards to feet.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which measurement describes volume?
{
~A. 5 feet#Feet measure length. Volume measures space inside a container.
=B. 2 gallons#Correct. Gallons measure volume.
~C. 12 square feet#Square feet measure area, not volume.
~D. 30 minutes#Minutes measure time. Match the unit to volume.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 96 inches to feet.
{
~A. 96 feet#This changes only the label. Divide inches by 12 to convert to feet.
~B. 12 feet#This uses 12 as the answer instead of dividing 96 by 12.
~C. 16 feet#This divides by 6, not by the 12 inches in one foot.
=D. 8 feet#Correct. Since 12 inches = 1 foot, 96 divided by 12 = 8 feet.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which statement is a correct conversion fact?
{
~A. 1 foot = 3 yards#This reverses the size relationship. A yard is longer than a foot.
=B. 1 yard = 3 feet#Correct. This equality can be used to convert between yards and feet.
~C. 1 inch = 12 feet#This reverses the inches-to-feet relationship. One foot has 12 inches.
~D. 1 hour = 100 minutes#Time is not base ten here. One hour has 60 minutes.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 5 hours to minutes.
{
=A. 300 minutes#Correct. Since 1 hour = 60 minutes, 5 x 60 = 300 minutes.
~B. 65 minutes#This adds 60 and 5 instead of multiplying. Each hour has 60 minutes.
~C. 120 minutes#This only represents 2 hours. Use all 5 hours in the conversion.
~D. 500 minutes#This treats an hour as 100 minutes. Use 60 minutes per hour.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which unit is best for measuring the weight of a backpack?
{
~A. inches#Inches measure length. A backpack's weight needs a weight unit.
~B. gallons#Gallons measure volume. They do not measure how heavy something is.
~C. square feet#Square feet measure area. Weight uses units such as pounds.
=D. pounds#Correct. Pounds measure weight and fit the situation.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 10 feet to inches.
{
~A. 22 inches#This adds 12 and 10 instead of multiplying by 12. Each foot has 12 inches.
~B. 100 inches#This treats a foot as 10 inches. Use 12 inches per foot.
=C. 120 inches#Correct. Since 1 foot = 12 inches, 10 x 12 = 120 inches.
~D. 12 inches#This converts only 1 foot. The measurement has 10 feet.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which measurement describes length?
{
=A. 14 inches#Correct. Inches measure length.
~B. 6 pounds#Pounds measure weight. Length needs a unit such as inches, feet, or yards.
~C. 4 gallons#Gallons measure volume. They do not measure distance or length.
~D. 30 minutes#Minutes measure time. Time is not length.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 2.5 hours to minutes.
{
~A. 25 minutes#This treats hours as tenths of a minute. Use 60 minutes per hour.
=B. 150 minutes#Correct. 2.5 x 60 = 150 minutes.
~C. 125 minutes#This uses 50 minutes per hour. The correct conversion is 60 minutes per hour.
~D. 250 minutes#This treats an hour as 100 minutes. Time conversions use 60 minutes per hour.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A table is 72 inches long. How many feet long is it?
{
~A. 6 inches#This gives the correct number but not the target unit. The answer should be feet.
~B. 12 feet#This uses the conversion number as the answer. Divide 72 by 12.
=C. 6 feet#Correct. Since 12 inches = 1 foot, 72 divided by 12 = 6 feet.
~D. 84 feet#This adds 12 instead of converting. Use division when changing inches to feet.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which unit would most likely measure the area of a small poster?
{
=A. square inches#Correct. A poster's flat surface area can be measured in square inches.
~B. inches#Inches measure length, not area. Area uses square units.
~C. gallons#Gallons measure volume. A poster is not measured by capacity.
~D. minutes#Minutes measure time. They do not describe flat space.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 6 yards to feet.
{
~A. 2 feet#This divides by 3, but yards to feet requires multiplying by 3.
~B. 6 feet#This leaves the number unchanged. A yard is longer than a foot.
~C. 12 feet#This doubles instead of using 3 feet per yard.
=D. 18 feet#Correct. Since 1 yard = 3 feet, 6 x 3 = 18 feet.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
When converting from feet to inches, what should usually happen to the number?
{
~A. It should get smaller#Feet are larger than inches, so more inches are needed for the same length.
=B. It should get larger#Correct. Changing from a larger unit to a smaller unit usually increases the number.
~C. It should become zero#A unit conversion keeps the same measurement amount, not zero.
~D. It should always stay the same#The real-world size stays the same, but the number changes with the unit.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 240 minutes to hours.
{
~A. 24 hours#This divides by 10 instead of 60. One hour has 60 minutes.
~B. 300 hours#This adds 60 instead of converting. Use division by 60.
=C. 4 hours#Correct. Since 60 minutes = 1 hour, 240 divided by 60 = 4 hours.
~D. 14400 hours#This multiplies by 60, which moves in the wrong direction for minutes to hours.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which pair of measurements is equivalent?
{
~A. 2 feet and 2 inches#These use different unit sizes and are not equal.
~B. 1 yard and 12 feet#One yard is 3 feet, not 12 feet.
~C. 1 hour and 30 minutes#One hour is 60 minutes, not 30 minutes.
=D. 3 feet and 36 inches#Correct. Since 1 foot = 12 inches, 3 feet = 36 inches.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which measurement is most reasonable for the capacity of a bucket?
{
=A. 5 gallons#Correct. Gallons measure volume or capacity.
~B. 5 feet#Feet measure length, not how much a container holds.
~C. 5 pounds#Pounds measure weight. Capacity needs a volume unit.
~D. 5 square inches#Square inches measure area, not capacity.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 15 feet to yards.
{
~A. 45 yards#This multiplies by 3, but feet to yards requires dividing by 3.
=B. 5 yards#Correct. Since 3 feet = 1 yard, 15 divided by 3 = 5 yards.
~C. 12 yards#This subtracts 3 instead of converting. Use the feet-per-yard relationship.
~D. 18 yards#This adds 3 instead of converting. Convert by dividing by 3.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which dimension is measured by square units?
{
~A. length#Length uses linear units such as feet or inches.
~B. time#Time uses units such as seconds or minutes.
~C. weight#Weight uses units such as pounds.
=D. area#Correct. Area measures flat space and uses square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 7 pounds to ounces if 1 pound = 16 ounces.
{
~A. 23 ounces#This adds 16 and 7 instead of multiplying. Each pound has 16 ounces.
~B. 70 ounces#This treats a pound as 10 ounces. Use 16 ounces per pound.
=C. 112 ounces#Correct. Since 1 pound = 16 ounces, 7 x 16 = 112 ounces.
~D. 16 ounces#This converts only 1 pound. The measurement has 7 pounds.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q21
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which conversion keeps the same real-world length?
{
=A. 4 feet = 48 inches#Correct. Since 1 foot = 12 inches, 4 feet equals 48 inches.
~B. 4 feet = 4 inches#This keeps the same number but changes to a much smaller unit, so the length changes.
~C. 4 feet = 16 inches#This uses 4 as the multiplier instead of 12 inches per foot.
~D. 4 feet = 24 inches#This converts only 2 feet. Four feet require 4 groups of 12 inches.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q22
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which operation converts 360 seconds to minutes if 60 seconds = 1 minute?
{
~A. 360 x 60#This would make the number much larger. Seconds to minutes should use division by 60.
=B. 360 divided by 60#Correct. Divide by 60 because 60 seconds make 1 minute.
~C. 360 + 60#Adding does not convert the unit. Use the equal-unit relationship.
~D. 360 - 60#Subtracting once does not convert seconds to minutes. Divide by the number of seconds in one minute.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q23
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which unit should label the answer after converting 8 yards to feet?
{
~A. yards#The target unit is feet, so the final label should not stay yards.
~B. inches#Inches are not the requested unit. Convert yards directly to feet.
=C. feet#Correct. The problem asks for feet, so the final unit is feet.
~D. square feet#Square feet measure area, but the problem converts length.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q24
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Convert 48 ounces to pounds if 16 ounces = 1 pound.
{
~A. 16 pounds#This uses the conversion number as the answer. Divide 48 by 16.
~B. 32 pounds#This subtracts 16 once instead of converting.
~C. 768 pounds#This multiplies by 16, which goes the wrong direction from ounces to pounds.
=D. 3 pounds#Correct. Since 16 ounces = 1 pound, 48 divided by 16 = 3 pounds.
}
::MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L01_Quiz_Q25
MLA Standard: Support-only embedded MLA.MCR.MC standards - No primary Unit 6 GM standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A hallway is 5 yards long. How many inches long is it?
{
=A. 180 inches#Correct. 5 yards = 15 feet, and 15 feet = 180 inches.
~B. 15 inches#This converts yards to feet but labels the answer as inches too soon.
~C. 60 inches#This treats each yard as 12 inches. First convert yards to feet or use 36 inches per yard.
~D. 300 inches#This uses 60 inches per yard. The correct relationship is 36 inches per yard.
}
